¶
'''atonable''' (ạ-tō′na̤-bl), ''a.'' [< ''atone'' + ''-able.''] Capable of being atoned for; reconcilable.

'''atone†''', ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' [ME., also ''attone'', earlier ''atoon'', ''aton'', ''at one'', ''at on'', lit. at one, agreed. In mod. use written as two words, ''at one'': see ''at'' and ''one''. In ''at-one'', as in ''al-one'' and ''on-ly'', ''one'' preserves its proper pronunciation (ōn), the usual pronunciation (wun) being a modern (16th century) corruption, which has not affected the compounds.] '''1.''' At one; reconciled.
{{smaller block|Make the wel ''at on'' with him . . . and dred the of the dome.
{{right|''Early Eng. Psalter'', p. 152.}}
''Aton'' he was with the king.
{{right|''King Horn.''}}
<poem>If gentil men, or othere of his contree,
Were wrothe, she wolde bringen hem ''atoon.''</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Clerk’s Tale, l. 381.}}}}
'''2.''' Together; at once.
{{smaller block|All his sences seemd berefte ''attone.''
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. i. 42.}}}}

'''atone''' (ạ-tōn′), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''atoned'', ppr. ''atoning.'' [< ''atone'', ''adv.'', q. v.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1†.''' To be at one; agree; be in accordance; accord.
{{smaller block|<poem>He and Aufidius can no more ''atone'',
Than violentest contrariety.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Cor., iv. 6.}}}}
'''2.''' To make reparation, amends, or satisfaction, as for an offense or a crime, or for an offender: with ''for''.
{{smaller block|The murderer fell, and blood ''atoned for'' blood.
{{right|''Pope.''}}
The ministry not ''atoning for'' their former conduct by any wise or popular measure.
{{right|''Junius.''}}
So it sometimes happens that a single bright and generous act serves to ''atone for'' the abuse of years.
{{right|''J. F. Clarke'', Self-Culture, p. 81.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
verb, arising perhaps from the phrase ''at onement'': see ''onement''.] '''1†.''' Reconciliation after enmity or controversy; settlement, as of a difference; concord.
{{smaller block|Hauying more regarde to their old variaunce than their newe ''attonement''. ''Sir T. More'', Descrip. of Rich. III.
<poem>If we do now make our ''atonement'' well,
Our peace will, like a broken limb united,
Grow stronger for the breaking.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iv. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' Satisfaction or reparation made for wrong or injury, either by giving some equivalent or by doing or suffering something which is received in lieu of an equivalent.
{{smaller block|<poem>O when did a morning shine
So rich in ''atonement'' as this
For my dark-dawning youth?</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Maud, xix. 2.}}}}
'''3.''' In ''theol.'', the reconciliation of God and man by means of the life, sufferings, and death of Christ.
{{smaller block|For God was in Christ, and made agrement bitwene the worlde and hym sylfe, and imputed not their synnes vnto them; and hath committed to vs the preachynge of the ''atonement''. {{right|''Tyndale'', 2 Cor. v. 19.}}

When we were enemies we were reconciled to God by the death of his Son; . . . we also joy in God through our Lord Jesus Christ, by whom we have now received the ''atonement''. {{right|Rom. v. 10, 11.}}}}
{{smaller block|This doctrine assumes that sin has made a spiritual separation between God and the human soul. Different systems of theology explain differently the method of reconciliation, and therefore use the word ''atonement'' with dif-}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|ferent meanings. The early fathers generally stated the doctrine in the terms of Scripture, and it was not until the time of the Reformation that the differences in philosophical statement were clearly marked. The modern statements may be grouped under four general heads, as follows: (''a'') A reparation or satisfaction for sin made by the sufferings of Christ as a substitute for the sinner, and in lieu of the punishment to which the sinner was justly amenable. Such satisfaction is regarded as necessary either (1) to satisfy the justice of God, and so make forgiveness possible, or (2) to satisfy the law of God, produce the public impression which punishment would have produced, and so make forgiveness safe. The former is known as the ''satisfaction'', the latter as the ''governmental theory''.}}

{{smaller block|The word ''atonement'', in its original sense, always denotes some amends, or satisfaction, for the neglect of some duty, or the commission of some fault; a satisfaction with which, when supposed to be complete, the person injured ought reasonably to be contented, and to demand of the offender nothing more on account of his transgression.
{{right|''Dwight'', Theology, lv.}}}}

{{smaller block|Taking the term ''atonement'' in its technical signification to denote the satisfaction of divine justice for the sin of man, by the substituted penal sufferings of the Son of God, we shall find a slower scientific unfolding of this great cardinal doctrine than of any other of the principal truths of Christianity.
{{right|''Shedd'', Hist. Christian Doctrine, v. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''b'') The entrance of God into humanity, that he may thereby drive out sin and make the human race at one with himself.}}

